ring.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Skin reactions are the most frequently reported adverse effect of quinolones\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Among all documented adverse reactions to quinolone antibiotics, skin-related manifestations—ranging from general skin reactions to specific allergic responses—are the most prevalent. One study of 84 patients found skin reactions in 33.3% of cases, making it the leading category of adverse events [Data: Relationships (10375)]. Another analysis of 42 patients similarly identified skin reactions as the predominant issue, accounting for 33.33% of all reported adverse effects [Data: Relationships (10376)]. These reactions include rashes, urticaria, and, in severe cases, anaphylactic shock or angioedema, particularly in individuals with prior allergy history [Data: Entities (16571, 16593); Relationships (6007)]. The high incidence suggests that dermatological monitoring should be prioritized during quinolone therapy.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Neurological and psychiatric risks necessitate caution in at-risk populations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Quinolone antibiotics are associated with central nervous system (CNS) adverse effects, including symptoms classified under '神经系统反应' (nervous system reactions), which occurred in 21.4% of adverse reaction cases in one cohort [Data: Relationships (10375, 10388)]. These may include dizziness, headaches, insomnia, and, more seriously, seizures or exacerbation of psychiatric conditions. Consequently, the drug class is explicitly contraindicated in patients with a history of epilepsy or psychiatric disorders [Data: Relationships (6006)]. This contraindication aligns with pharmacological evidence indicating that certain quinolones, especially fourth-generation agents, can lower seizure thresholds or interfere with GABAergic neurotransmission. Clinicians must screen for neurological and psychiatric history before initiating therapy.\"